{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Toronto Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"","formatted_current_value2":"2.00","formatted_progress_value2":"2.00","formatted_target_value2":"0.00","measure_header_Text2":"Number of Stage IV Hospital Acquired Pressure Injuries (never event)","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Unity Health Toronto","planned_initiative":"Complete formal patient safety incident reviews of all Stage 4 pressure injuries using common cause analysis.","process_measure":"The Unity Pressure Ulcer Steering Committee provides corporate oversight for organization wide pressure injury reduction strategy. Due to implementation of new EPR system the committee paused work during Q3 and Q4. However, integration of strategies to support best practices in pressure injury prevention were part of the build and education on the new EPR. 

Common cause analysis of stage 4 hospital acquired pressure injury completed. Analysis identified common causes of resource allocation (e.g. pillows, air mattresses), delay to wound care referral and communications especially during unit transfers.

Continued efforts are required into the next year on to ensure the right infrastructure is in place to review and support the overall hospital acquired pressure injury strategy including incident reporting, equipment and resources.","progress2":"At the end of Q3 2024-25 we had 2 hospital acquired stage 4 pressure injuries. This is above our target of zero.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"East Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Chronic/Rehab Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"81.30","formatted_progress_value2":"85.30","formatted_target_value2":"84.20","measure_header_Text2":"Patient experience: Would you recommend this hospital to family or friends if they needed this type of care?","methods":"N","org_name_Text2":"Bruyère Health","planned_initiative":"Establishment of Nursing Always Practices Steering Committee with primary focus on sustainability strategies including a) Supporting facilitation of safety huddles b) Addressing barriers of bedside shift report","process_measure":"a) Patient assignment pilot not implemented on other units due to feedback from clinical staff and leadership related to challenges with shifting schedules. Focus on teaching related to BSSR on various units especially HEAL checklist.

b) A total of 249 audits completed to date for fiscal year. Target was 350 audits for Q1-3, therefore 71% of target was met. 

Percentage of patients who respond always to the question "Do you see your nurse on a regular basis?" in the patient experience survey is 70% which is above target.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Central Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"31.00","formatted_progress_value2":"32.00","formatted_target_value2":"26.00","measure_header_Text2":"Number of workplace violence incidents reported by hospital workers (as defined by OHSA) within a 12 month period, that result in Lost Time or Healthcare.","methods":"N","org_name_Text2":"Southlake Regional Health Centre","planned_initiative":"Develop a Workplace Violence Prevention awareness campaign Workplace Violence Prevention (WPVP)program helps increase understanding and appreciation for the importance of the program across the organization. Increased understanding of hospital protocols and policies on workplace violence helps create a safer and more resilient work environment. In addition, the program also helps increase accountability by building capacity across the organization to make it more reliable.","process_measure":"Successes for this change idea included collaboration with key stakeholders to re-structure the WPVP committee. However, barriers to this change idea were that resources were not allocated to the campaign / alternate priorities as an organization.","progress2":"Successfully revamped membership and terms of reference of the WPVP committee.   



Awareness Campaign deferred to May 2025 (H&S week) as per Corp Comms due to optics.  

Efforts continue to push leaders to complete SPOT checks.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Central Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"31.00","formatted_progress_value2":"32.00","formatted_target_value2":"26.00","measure_header_Text2":"Number of workplace violence incidents reported by hospital workers (as defined by OHSA) within a 12 month period, that result in Lost Time or Healthcare.","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Southlake Regional Health Centre","planned_initiative":"Increase the frequency of Public Services Health and Safety Association (PSHSA) Risk Assessments Completing frequent and timely risk assessments is essential in minimizing then number of workplace violence incidents reported by hospital workers, as defined by OSHA, over a 12-month period that lead to lost time or healthcare needs. These assessments proactively identify and mitigate potential hazards, ensuring a safer environment for employees. By thoroughly analyzing and addressing risks, hospitals can implement effective strategies to prevent violent incidents, thereby safeguarding workers and minimizing disruptions in healthcare delivery due to lost time or injuries. This proactive approach not only enhances the safety and well-being of hospital staff but also contributes to amore stable and efficient healthcare system.","process_measure":"Successes for this change idea included stakeholder active engagement in both the training and the completion of their risk assessments. What enabled this was having support from ELT. However, a barrier to this change idea was competing priorities for unit level leadership","progress2":"Successfully revamped membership and terms of reference of the WPVP committee.   



Awareness Campaign deferred to May 2025 (H&S week) as per Corp Comms due to optics.  

Efforts continue to push leaders to complete SPOT checks.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Central Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"78.50","formatted_progress_value2":"83.90","formatted_target_value2":"80.10","measure_header_Text2":"Were you involved in your care in a way that you wished? - Yes, definitely","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"William Osler Health System","planned_initiative":"Achievement of RNAO People Centred Care (PCC) Best Practice Guidelines (BPG) Year 1-2 deliverables. Documentation of: 1) Inpatient Nursing Assessment Question: "What is most important to you today? 2) Inclusion of family presence and reinforcement of admission goal during Nursing Bedside Report.","process_measure":"Challenge: below target (35.4%) re: question - What is most important to you today? in Inpatient Admission Assessment. 
Success: Documentation of family involvement at bedside report - 84.4%

Process Measures and targets to be re-evaluated in partnership with Professional Practice and OP.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"West Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"16.40","formatted_progress_value2":"16.50","formatted_target_value2":"5.00","measure_header_Text2":"Emergency Department Time to Inpatient Bed (TTIB)","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Chatham-Kent Health Alliance","planned_initiative":"Create a fulsome Quality Improvement project with dedicated resources and focus.","process_measure":"- Revisions to morning bed meeting format to focus on discharges and escalation to support challenging discharges.  

