{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"West Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"68.80","formatted_progress_value2":"64.14","formatted_target_value2":"72.20","measure_header_Text2":"Communication with Physicians and Nurses (%) - Composite Metric","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Grand River Hospital Corporation","planned_initiative":"Continue implementation of customer service training in priority areas","process_measure":"An e-learning module was made available to the entire organization and staff working in the Emergency Department and Inpatient Stroke Unit were identified as key areas of focus.

The module was well received by staff and sets a good baseline of expectations for new staff during onboarding.

Ongoing training opportunities which are focused to specific clinical areas and scenarios will continue to be explored.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"West Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"0.29","formatted_progress_value2":"0.07","formatted_target_value2":"0.22","measure_header_Text2":"Rate of workplace violence incidents resulting in lost time injury","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Grand River Hospital Corporation","planned_initiative":"Continue implementation of recommendations from workplace violence program audit.","process_measure":"100% of the violence risk assessments were completed. Violence Risk assessments help the organization determine if units are appropriately labelled as high risk to ensure team members have the tools they need to mitigate violence (i.e., training).

Documented leadership follow up on incident reports including corrective action is inconsistent.

Communication challenges were faced with the integration, due to information now going to both organizations and wanting to ensure the delivery of GRH specific safety information was still received and visible to our team members.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"West Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"X","formatted_progress_value2":"28.00","formatted_target_value2":"100.00","measure_header_Text2":"Percentage of staff (all leadership) who have completed relevant equity, diversity, inclusion, and anti-racism education","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"St. Mary's General Hospital","planned_initiative":"All Leadership staff will have completed relevant EDI & Anti-racism education.","process_measure":"Integration of departments with GRH gave rise to new position, Manager of Equity, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI), with responsibility for creating educational material.

Development and approval of course material required time, so many modules were not ready until Q3, which left little time for leadership to complete the modules.

Multiple e-learning modules developed for mandatory leadership completion by Mar 31, 2025.  Multiple in-person learning sessions were scheduled, which aided in ensuring completion of this training; however, due to limited capacity of facilitators and group size, not all leaders were able to attend in-person trainings.  Offering one of the sessions during an organization-wide leadership day was another way to enable training of all leaders by providing the education during another event most were attending.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"West Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"16.50","formatted_progress_value2":"16.95","formatted_target_value2":"20.00","measure_header_Text2":"Rate of delirium beginning during hospitalization","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"St. Mary's General Hospital","planned_initiative":"Per Choosing Wisely and Senior Friendly Framework","process_measure":"Much of the work related to delirium and the provincial DASH program was tied to other QIP indicators.  See lessons learned for Senior Friendly Framework and Choosing Wisely bundles.

Initial development of a delirium dashboard to help view/monitor various indicators was a good start to understanding where to focus our delirium work.  This also required a lot of time and support from decision support to develop indicators and determine how to gather the data in Cerner.

Education and awareness of DASH work increased delirium rates (as expected).  Further review of Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) assessment training and completion is anticipated to lead to more completed assessments and likely higher rates of delirium onset simply due to assessments being completed more frequently.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

