{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Central Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"4.77","formatted_progress_value2":"4.80","formatted_target_value2":"4.53","measure_header_Text2":"90th percentile emergency department wait time to physician initial assessment","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Halton Healthcare Services Corporation","planned_initiative":"Rapid Assessment Fast Track (RAFT) Optimization (Net new at OTMH, MDH & GH optimization)","process_measure":"Key learnings included the importance of sequencing changes based on site readiness, maintaining leadership stability, and ensuring consistent physician and nursing engagement throughout the change cycle. The most effective strategies were supported by strong tri-site governance, disciplined PDSA methodology, and access to reliable, real-time data. Barriers identified included variation in physician alignment, inconsistent adoption pacing, and less visible contributors to delay such as consultation bottlenecks and inpatient bed assignment lag. These findings reinforced the need for standardized consult expectations and navigation supports.","progress2":"While year-over-year results as populated by Ontario Health shows performance declined by less than 1% based on the Ontario Health timeline,  Halton Healthcare’s change ideas were planned for implementation starting in April 2025.  For FY 2025/26, year to date improvement demonstrates meaningful and sustained gains later in the year, particularly during periods of elevated volumes and acuity. The establishment of ED committees and action tables in the second quarter of the fiscal year contributed to rapid improvements, even as demand continued to grow. Additionally, the opening of the command center further enhanced operational oversight and coordination, supporting these positive trends.  

Most notably, in October and November, the organization consistently exceeded the corporate PIA target of under four hours, reflecting strengthened operational reliability during high-pressure months. However, late year gains were insufficient to offset PIA performance in Q4 of fiscal year 2024/2025.  

The streamlining of zones at OTMH and the optimization of Fast Track areas across the three ED sites had a clear and observable impact. OTMH realized the earliest and most sustained gains related to ED zone redesign, clearer workflow delineation, and empowered interprofessional teams—structural and cultural changes that drove earlier and more consistent reductions in PIA. MDH’s implementation of similar flow improvements started in December 2025, and produced early improvements through targeted flow interventions aligned to peak volume patterns. GH implemented phased improvements, including a mini cycling care area and optimized scheduling approaches, deliberately sequenced to align with local leadership readiness and minimize operational disruption. Collectively, these interventions improved throughput reliability and enhanced the organization’s capacity to absorb fluctuations in ED arrivals.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Central Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"22.69","formatted_progress_value2":"28.04","formatted_target_value2":"21.56","measure_header_Text2":"Daily average number of patients waiting in the emergency department for an inpatient bed at 8 a.m.","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Halton Healthcare Services Corporation","planned_initiative":"Increase referrals to outpatient clinics from the Emergency Department to avoid admission.","process_measure":"Key lessons identified across the initiatives summarized above.","progress2":"+NB: Ontario Health introduced NBA last year as a QIP indicator, and used previous performance data based on Q1 and Q2 in 2024 only. Current performance is based on the full fiscal year of 2024/25, which does not align with the QIP workplan for 2025/26, which started on April 1, 2025. This makes it an unreasonable measure of current performance based on QIP improvements. Since April 1, 2025, January YTD performance on NBA is 23.31, which shows a YTD improvement of 17% versus a reported 23.58% worsening of performance.  

To improve patient flow and reduce avoidable admissions, two key initiatives were implemented: a formal ED to Halton@Home admission avoidance pathway and expanded ED referrals to priority ambulatory clinics, which increased by 13.5% year over year. These pathways were embedded into ED workflows through clearer eligibility criteria and targeted case finding. Patient outcomes remained strong, with a 30-day readmission rate of approximately 5%, well below the <10% target, confirming safe and appropriate diversion to outpatient care. 

Of note, one process measure was incorrectly entered as a target of 20 referrals per week; the intended target was 20 referrals per month, with planned scaling as program capacity developed. As the program scaled, we made steady progress towards the target and were able to exceed our targets by the first month of Q4.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Central Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"0.64","formatted_progress_value2":"0.27","formatted_target_value2":"0.61","measure_header_Text2":"Rate of workplace violence incidents reported by hospital workers that resulted harm (as defined by OHSA) within a 12-month period.","methods":"N","org_name_Text2":"Mackenzie Health","planned_initiative":"Reduce workplace violence incidents resulting in healthcare and lost time injury.","process_measure":"1) Process measure completed although there are ongoing challenges include resources to facilitate de-escalation training. A business case has been approved to have dedicated resource to support virtual/online training in the next 2 years. 

2) Process measure completed. This continues to be embedded into the onboarding for new leaders. 

3) Process measure in progress as change idea owner continues to collaborate with professional practice to train staff on the assessment tool. Compliance is around 70-80%)","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Central Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"8.90","formatted_progress_value2":"8.90","formatted_target_value2":"9.00","measure_header_Text2":"Patient Experience "Rate your experience 0-10"","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Trillium Health Partners","planned_initiative":"Enhance knowledge and proficiency in the Qualtrics XM system by providing comprehensive education throughout THP","process_measure":"• Standardized onboarding and ongoing access to Qualtrics XM improved foundational capability across clinical leadership, with strong uptake observed among newer leaders who embedded use into routine practice.

• Engagement patterns highlight an opportunity to further integrate routine review of patient experience dashboards into standard leadership practices. 

• Learnings highlight the need to strengthen accountability structures to ensure routine review, discussion, and use of patient experience data as part of standard leadership expectations and performance management.","progress2":"Ongoing refinements to implementation approaches will further support organizational performance targets. In the Emergency Department, dedicated project support is currently advancing early engagement work, including team consultations, focus groups, and structured observations, with activities progressing as planned.  We will also expand tailored training for clinical educators as part of FY2026/27 workplan.  See 2026/27 workplan for additional details.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

