{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"North East Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Small Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"15","formatted_progress_value2":"16","formatted_target_value2":"14","measure_header_Text2":"90th percentile ambulance offload time","methods":"N","org_name_Text2":"Notre Dame Hospital (Hearst)","planned_initiative":"Continue working with our Ambulance Services","process_measure":"We made steady efforts to improve our results, but we did not reach the one‑minute improvement target. 

This indicator is inherently difficult to measure with precision because the data is collected by an external third‑party organization, which limits our ability to validate or refine the methodology. 

Despite this challenge, our performance continues to remain well below the provincial benchmark of 30 minutes, indicating that our overall response time is still strong.","progress2":"Given that this indicator continues to perform well below the provincial target and our overall results remain strong, we have chosen to shift our focus to other priority indicators for the coming year. We will, however, continue to monitor this measure internally to maintain visibility on its trends and ensure sustained performance.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Toronto Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Specialty Children Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"103.00","formatted_progress_value2":"149.00","formatted_target_value2":"98.00","measure_header_Text2":"Total number of staff events resulting in first aid, lost time, and healthcare, following a workplace violence incident.","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Hospital for Sick Children (The)","planned_initiative":"Increase Utilization of Comfort & Safety Bundle.","process_measure":"To enhance the screening process for potential patient escalations, the Comfort and Safety Bundle (CSB) has seen increased utilization across inpatient areas. Over the course of the year, compliance has continued to rise allowing for more effective identification of patients who are at higher risk of behavioural escalation and heightened staff awareness and safety. We expect this compliance rate to continue improving across inpatient areas. The team is focused on rolling out the CSB into outpatient workflows, as well as enhancing compliance with the completion of care plans for patients at higher risk of escalation.","progress2":"The number of workplace violence events resulting in first aid, lost time, or healthcare increased significantly this year, rising from 103 to 149, which is above the target of 98.  

Despite this overall increase, the most severe incidents—those resulting in Lost Time—remained very low, decreasing slightly from 5 in 2024 to 4 in 2025 

Growing patient complexity and acuity has likely contributed to the rise in reported events. Code White volumes increased by 137% compared to the previous year, with Unit 7A (Adolescent Medicine, Psychiatry) experiencing a 284% increase—an outcome possibly linked to its transition to a Schedule 1 facility under the Mental Health Act, where many involuntary patients were previously redirected to other hospitals are now admitted. The number of patients with repeat events has also increased, reflecting shifts in the nature of the patient population. Between December 2024 and September 2025, approximately 40% of incidents involved patients who escalated four or more times, and about 60% involved patients with three or more escalations.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Toronto Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Specialty Children Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"103.00","formatted_progress_value2":"149.00","formatted_target_value2":"98.00","measure_header_Text2":"Total number of staff events resulting in first aid, lost time, and healthcare, following a workplace violence incident.","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Hospital for Sick Children (The)","planned_initiative":"Review all employee safety reports related to aggressive/ violent action to ensure contributing factors and preventative measures are identified and implemented.","process_measure":"The review of employee safety reports provided valuable insights into recurring patterns and trends, helping us strengthen our approach to risk mitigation and support for staff during escalated events. The data showed that many incidents involved a small number of patients displaying common behaviours, which is informing ongoing work related to behavioural PPE and prevention strategies. Continued emphasis on reporting will help ensure we maintain a comprehensive understanding of incident trends and can proactively refine our safety practices.","progress2":"The number of workplace violence events resulting in first aid, lost time, or healthcare increased significantly this year, rising from 103 to 149, which is above the target of 98.  

Despite this overall increase, the most severe incidents—those resulting in Lost Time—remained very low, decreasing slightly from 5 in 2024 to 4 in 2025 

Growing patient complexity and acuity has likely contributed to the rise in reported events. Code White volumes increased by 137% compared to the previous year, with Unit 7A (Adolescent Medicine, Psychiatry) experiencing a 284% increase—an outcome possibly linked to its transition to a Schedule 1 facility under the Mental Health Act, where many involuntary patients were previously redirected to other hospitals are now admitted. The number of patients with repeat events has also increased, reflecting shifts in the nature of the patient population. Between December 2024 and September 2025, approximately 40% of incidents involved patients who escalated four or more times, and about 60% involved patients with three or more escalations.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Toronto Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Specialty Mental Health Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"64.00","formatted_progress_value2":"49.00","formatted_target_value2":"51.00","measure_header_Text2":"Median Wait Time from Referral to First Offered Consult Appointment.","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Centre for Addiction and Mental Health","planned_initiative":"Scale and spread wait time reduction approach to an additional clinic within CAMH","process_measure":"The team was able to onboard 2 new clinics during the year so far with significant improvement to their wait times. 

Adult Neurodevelopment Services (ANS) was onboarded and saw data capture improve from 46% to 92%, and wait times dropped by 36% from Q1 to Q2. 

Child and Adolescent Psychiatric Assessment Clinic (FAST) was onboarded as well and achieved an 11.36% decrease in wait times.","progress2":"Continue to include this indicator on next QIP with progression in change ideas.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Toronto Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Specialty Mental Health Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"0.18","formatted_progress_value2":"0.31","formatted_target_value2":"0.28","measure_header_Text2":"Workplace Violence Lost Time Injury Frequency (# of WPV incidents/100 FTEs)","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Centre for Addiction and Mental Health","planned_initiative":"Evaluating and maintaining TIDES education for clinical spaces.","process_measure":"Through our consistent TIDES training the staff continued to show a marked increase in both knowledge and confidence after their training session. Since April, the team has received 1,150 evaluations with positive results. 

Reported an increase in knowledge – range between 80-88% 

Reported an increase in confidence – range between 76-77%","progress2":"Continue to include this indicator on next QIP with progression in change ideas.","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"East Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Teaching Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"59.70","formatted_progress_value2":"56.00","formatted_target_value2":"65.00","measure_header_Text2":"Pourcentage de répondants qui ont répondu « complètement » à la question suivante : Croyez-vous qu’il y avait une bonne communication à propos de vos soins entre les médecins, les infirmiers et les infirmières et les autres membres du personnel de l’hôpital?","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Hopital Montfort","planned_initiative":"Réaliser une analyse de corrélation afin d'identifier les questions du sondage ayant le plus d'impact sur la perception de la communication des patients.","process_measure":"L’analyse de corrélation menée cette année a permis d’identifier les éléments du sondage ayant le plus d’impact sur la perception des patients en matière de communication. Les résultats de cet exercice ont été partagés avec les parties concernées afin d’informer les actions à venir. 



Il ressort de cette analyse que, de façon générale, l’expérience des patients à l’égard du respect et de la courtoisie demeure positive. Cependant, un constat important émerge : plusieurs patients mentionnent ne pas recevoir suffisamment d’information pendant leur séjour. Les questions liées à cet aspect affichent une forte corrélation avec l’expérience globale de communication, ce qui met en lumière l’importance de la transmission claire et complète des renseignements aux patients. 



Face à ce constat, une démarche de sensibilisation a été entreprise auprès du personnel. L’accent a été mis sur la nécessité de valider la compréhension des patients, notamment par l’utilisation de questions ouvertes. Cette approche vise à assurer que l’information communiquée soit effectivement comprise et réponde adéquatement aux besoins exprimés par les patients.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"East Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Teaching Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"59.70","formatted_progress_value2":"56.00","formatted_target_value2":"65.00","measure_header_Text2":"Pourcentage de répondants qui ont répondu « complètement » à la question suivante : Croyez-vous qu’il y avait une bonne communication à propos de vos soins entre les médecins, les infirmiers et les infirmières et les autres membres du personnel de l’hôpital?","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Hopital Montfort","planned_initiative":"Réviser le programme de formation en lien avec la communication pour l'aligner sur les meilleures pratiques et intégrer les enseignements tirés des sondages et des commentaires des patients.","process_measure":"Le comité a procédé à une révision approfondie des formations existantes afin de garantir leur pertinence et de s'assurer qu'elles tiennent compte des commentaires recueillis auprès des patients, des proches ainsi que des rapports d'incident. Cette démarche visait à aligner le contenu des formations avec les besoins exprimés par les différentes parties prenantes. 



À la suite de cette analyse, le comité a décidé de déployer une nouvelle formation Osoin  qui s'harmonise avec la manière renouvelée de se présenter et d'interagir avec les patients et les partenaires de l'organisation. Cette approche met l'accent sur la sensibilité à l'équité, à la diversité et à l'inclusion, favorisant ainsi une offre active des services en anglais et en français, un environnement accueillant et respectueux pour tous. 



La formation s'appuie sur divers scénarios illustrant le quotidien de plusieurs groupes au sein de l'organisation. Elle est accessible via une plateforme en ligne, ce qui permet d'assurer un suivi rigoureux des participants et de garantir la conformité des audits.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"East Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Teaching Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"35.20","formatted_progress_value2":"27.32","formatted_target_value2":"30.00","measure_header_Text2":"Percentage of people with Heart Failure who get admitted or return to ED within 30 days after HF admission and being optimized by the HF physician assistant.","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"Kingston Health Sciences Centre","planned_initiative":"Optimize heart failure patients during their hospital stay, improve care transition, and coordinate with community partner.","process_measure":"Physician assistant saw and optimized the patients every quarter. The patients were provided education and connected to Ontario Health at Home for follow up care. 
Physician Assistant also followed up via call for >90% of HF patients upon discharge

The target was met for eligible patients being fast tracked and seen by HF specialist physician in less than 14 days.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"East Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Teaching Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"CB","formatted_progress_value2":"CB","formatted_target_value2":"CB","measure_header_Text2":"Improving the care and safety of patients, in particular transgender (trans) and non-binary patients","methods":"N","org_name_Text2":"The Ottawa Hospital","planned_initiative":"Adoption of the Organ (Anatomic) Inventory tool in the Electronic Medical Record within the Department of Medical Imaging (DMI)","process_measure":"% DMI clinical staff satisfaction on survey - majority are satisfied: N/A

There has been uptake of the Respecting Gender Diversity ELM by the DMI team; leaders have continued encouraging staff to complete at the same time as they complete mandatory training such as WHMIS etc. Since implementation, over 5000 patients have completed the organ inventory, with greatest completion rates in MRI, Ultrasound and Mammography.
This initiative was highlighted under the pillar of "nurture our social responsibility" on the 25/26 strategic plan, however, the ELM is expected and encouraged, but not mandatory for employees. While there has been great uptake, having it be part of the ELMs would have helped increase the number of staff who completed the ELMs.","progress2":"","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

