{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Toronto Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Teaching Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"1.11","formatted_progress_value2":"1.03","formatted_target_value2":"1.00","measure_header_Text2":"Surgical Backlog Clearance Rate","methods":"N","org_name_Text2":"University Health Network","planned_initiative":"1) Operational efficiencies and optimized Models of Care 2) Utilize MIS to increase Day Sx volumes. 3) Continued exploration of Day Sx partnerships. 4) Central intake and wait list load-sharing.","process_measure":"1. Sustained throughput above target. UHN consistently achieved a clearance rate >1.0, indicating the backlog was shrinking when incremental strategies were in place. 

2. Incremental OR time Adding incremental OR time, particularly for oncology long‑wait patients, resulted in meaningful case completions and protected access for time‑sensitive care. This was most effective when aligned to priority populations and sustained over multiple months rather than short bursts. 

3. Community partnerships expand system capacity beyond the hospital walls. The UHN–Humber River Health surgical capacity partnership enabled the offloading of benign cases, directly reducing specific backlogs (e.g., hernia and benign urology) while freeing tertiary capacity at UHN. This reinforced the “right care, right place” model as a viable and scalable approach.","progress2":"tbd","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"West Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Teaching Hospital","formatted_current_value2":"CB","formatted_progress_value2":"159.00","formatted_target_value2":"CB","measure_header_Text2":"To decrease occurrences of violence and aggression demonstrated by patients across 4 inpatient units by 10%","methods":"Y","org_name_Text2":"St. Joseph's Health Care System Hamilton","planned_initiative":"Collect data to understand the problem of workplace violence and identify four pilot sites","process_measure":"Staff (safety) Incident reporting (HIR) is key to the data that informs this QIP. However, the data is subjective in that peoples’ understanding and comfort in reporting incidents varies as well as capacity to document all incidents. It is recognized that the extent to which we have the necessary information to inform the QIP is limited. ​

Additionally, how HIRs are completed and what level of detail is provided and the accuracy of the components selected when documenting the HIR can be limited. ​

Healthcare workers need to be confident that there will be supervisory follow up after their report and that follow up is not punitive but could be supported with corrective actions​","progress2":"Continuing into year 2 QIP with new clinical areas","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital"}

