{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"Toronto Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Teaching Hospital","aim_header_Text2":"Equitable","comments2":"","current_value_text2":"0.00","datasource_period_Text2":"In-house survey / FY 2026-27","goal2":"• 36% of programs complete the Indigenous Cultural Safety Scorecard by Q4 2026/27 • 100% of participating programs identify at least one improvement opportunity and corresponding action • 100% of participating programs flagged for support receive follow-up engagement from Indigenous Health and Partnerships Lead within one quarter of scorecard completion","measure_header_Text2":"By March 31, 2027, increase organizational capacity to provide culturally safer care for First Nations, Inuit and Métis patients by improving staff knowledge, confidence, and system supports for Indigenous cultural and spiritual practices, as measured through participation in Indigenous safety education, access to cultural practices (e.g., smudging), and improved reporting and measurement related to spiritual, cultural, and emotional harm.Indicator: % of Care Programs having implemented the Indigenous Cultural Safety Scorecard.","methods2":"1. Design the scorecard in partnership with the Indigenous Advisory Council, Quality & Patient Safety, Office of the Patient Experience, Spiritual Care and Social Work, with input from frontline staff to ensure relevance, cultural safety, and feasibility • Include a mix of process, structural, and learning indicators, such as: o Completion of Indigenous cultural safety education o Access to and use of cultural/spiritual practices (e.g., smudging) o Unit awareness of reporting pathways for spiritual/cultural harm o Engagement with Indigenous Health supports and resources 2. Implement the scorecard as a self-assessment tool, completed by Program leadership 3. Embed the scorecard into existing quality review structures (e.g., quarterly quality huddles, leadership reviews, or unit councils) 4. Use scorecard results to trigger supportive follow-up, such as targeted education, coaching, or resource deployment","objective_header_Text2":"Equity","org_name_Text2":"Sunnybrook Health Sciences Centre","planned3":"Implement a Unit-Level Indigenous Cultural Safety Scorecard","process_measure3":"• % of programs that complete the Indigenous Cultural Safety Scorecard for at least one clinical area • % of units that identify at least one strength and one area for improvement • % of units receiving targeted follow-up support based on scorecard results","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital","target_justification2":"Scorecard yet to be developed.

• Aim is to have 4 of the 11 Care Programs with a scorecard in use by Q4 noting that the scorecard will be developed in partnership with the Indigenous Advisory Council
• The scorecard, once implemented, will support programs to identify future improvement opportunities","target_value_text2":"36.00","unit_population_Text2":"% / Other"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"West Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Large Community Hospital","aim_header_Text2":"Timely","comments2":"","current_value_text2":"45.00","datasource_period_Text2":"CIHI NACRS / FY 2024","goal2":"1. Greater than or equal to 90% of qualifying opioid orders reviewed within 24 hours and interchanged 2. Greater than or equal to 85% of eligible patients 3. establishing baseline 4. 100% of applicable inpatient units complete a 5S assessment, with great than or equal to 80% adherence to standardized decluttering criteria within 6 months.","measure_header_Text2":"Number of conservable bed days linked to Organic mental disorders (For this indicator, organic mental health means changes in thinking, awareness, or behavior that are caused by a medical or physical condition, rather than a mental illness like depression or anxiety. It includes problems such as dementia, confusion, agitation, pain, falls, delirium and other medical symptoms that often affect older adults and can lead to longer hospital stays if not treated early.)","methods2":"1. Implement an automated clinical pain-safety interchange whereby any order for =1 mg IV hydromorphone (Dilaudid) in patients =65 years triggers a standardized senior-friendly pain review. This includes dose reassessment, consideration of non-opioid or lower-dose alternatives, and delirium risk screening. 2. Standardize automatic dietitian referral for all inpatients =65 years within 24 hours of admission, with reassessment for prolonged length of stay or poor intake. 3. Reduce catheter-associated harm and support dignity, mobility, and timely discharge through the development of a medical directive 4. Improve safety, orientation, and mobility for older adults by reducing environmental clutter on inpatient units through a structured 5S (Sort, Set in Order, Shine, Standardize, Sustain) approach","objective_header_Text2":"Access and Flow","org_name_Text2":"Erie Shores Healthcare","planned3":"Reduce opioid-related delirium and falls in patients ≥65 years through standardized pain safety review","process_measure3":"1. Percentage of hydromorphone orders =1 mg in patients equal to or greater than 65 years that receive a documented pain safety review. 2. Percentage of admitted patients equal to or greater than 65 years who receive a dietitian referral within 24 hours of admission. 3. % of medical directives implemented % of foley reinserted within 24 hours after utilization of directive Average Foley catheter days per patient greater than or equal to 65 years 4. Percentage of inpatient units that have completed a documented 5S assessment and action plan","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital","target_justification2":"New Indicator
A 10% reduction in conservable bed days associated with organic mental disorders is an appropriate and achievable target given the strong linkage between cognitive syndromes (e.g., delirium, dementia, disorientation, agitation, falls, pain, and functional decline) and prolonged or avoidable inpatient length of stay. These conditions frequently represent potentially preventable or modifiable drivers of delayed discharge when early identification, standardized assessment, timely symptom management, and coordinated discharge planning are not consistently applied.

From an access and flow perspective, reducing conservable bed days directly increases effective inpatient capacity without additional physical beds. Earlier recognition and proactive management of delirium and related cognitive or functional syndromes support timely transitions to the most appropriate level of care (e.g., home with supports, rehabilitation, complex continuing care, or palliative pathways), thereby reducing downstream congestion, emergency department boarding, and delays in admission for patients awaiting inpatient beds. This aligns with system priorities to optimize flow across the continuum and improve emergency department throughput.","target_value_text2":"35.00","unit_population_Text2":"% / All inpatients"}

{"LHIN_Label_Text2":"West Region","OrgCategory_Label_Text2":"Small Hospital","aim_header_Text2":"Timely","comments2":"Formalizing an existing practice may help improve consistency. ED Chief of Staff to follow up with individuals as appropriate under professional oversight.","current_value_text2":"4.18","datasource_period_Text2":"CIHI NACRS / Other","goal2":"80% of relevant ED staff reviewed standardized process by end of Q3 Quarterly review completed Reporting completed in 80% of months.","measure_header_Text2":"90th percentile emergency department length of stay for nonadmitted patients with low acuity","methods2":"Document and standardize ED low-acuity processes Review ED LOS trends, including provider-level and day-of-week patterns, with the ED Chief of Staff","objective_header_Text2":"Access and Flow","org_name_Text2":"South Huron Hospital","planned3":"Formalize and standardize ED low-acuity flow processes and review performance trends","process_measure3":"Standardized low-acuity ED process documented and reviewed with ED clinical staff Quarterly ED LOS trend reports shared with ED Chief of Staff Monthly ED LOS trend reporting completed","sector_name_Text2":"Acute Care/Hospital","target_justification2":"Ontario Health’s benchmark for this indicator is 4 hours.  

Regional (South West) data shows similar performance patterns among very low-volume hospitals. 

The target of 5.0 hours reflects realistic, sustainable performance for comparable low-volume hospitals while supporting process improvements and incremental progress toward provincial expectations.","target_value_text2":"5.00","unit_population_Text2":"Hours / ED patients"}

